1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,900
A 2,000-year-old stone wall bisects rural England.

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:07,600
Was it once a fortress and a barrier,

3
00:00:07,900 --> 00:00:11,500
built by ancient invaders to mark the furthest reaches of their empire?

4
00:00:12,100 --> 00:00:13,800
Who was it designed to exclude?

5
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:15,600
And who did it protect?

6
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:20,000
Now, Hadrian's Wall on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:39,280
There was a seam that cuts through the patchwork fields and pastures of northern England.

8
00:00:42,420 --> 00:00:48,240
A rock and mortar scar, 80 Roman miles long and 2,000 years old.

9
00:00:48,241 --> 00:01:02,241
Hadrian's Wall persists as a relic of an ancient Roman past, chained to the boggy soil and

10
00:01:03,740 --> 00:01:07,440
linking our modern time to its ancient one.

11
00:01:07,441 --> 00:01:15,280
Mute, cold, weathered.

12
00:01:15,281 --> 00:01:29,281
Observer to an endless parade of kings and thieves, farmers and soldiers.

13
00:01:33,080 --> 00:01:35,120
Hadrian's Wall keeps many secrets.

14
00:01:35,920 --> 00:01:38,440
For something so solid, tangible and real,

15
00:01:39,020 --> 00:01:43,360
its early history peers tentatively from the vague corners of its past.

16
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:49,820
Roman history has left us with virtually no information about this northern frontier.

17
00:01:49,821 --> 00:01:52,820
I think there are two sentences in the whole of Roman history

18
00:01:52,821 --> 00:01:55,660
which actually refer to Hadrian's Wall, for example.

19
00:01:57,980 --> 00:02:00,040
According to one ancient historian,

20
00:02:00,041 --> 00:02:07,040
Hadrian was the first to build a wall 80 miles long to separate the Romans and the barbarians.

21
00:02:07,041 --> 00:02:12,040
They're right, it was 80 miles long, but he didn't build it to separate the Romans from the barbarians.

22
00:02:12,041 --> 00:02:16,040
I mean, there's just as many barbarians to the south of the wall as there are to the north.

23
00:02:18,040 --> 00:02:25,040
Nearly all we know of Hadrian's wall comes from the archaeological record of the wall and its environment.

24
00:02:25,041 --> 00:02:30,840
If you want to know about the structure of the wall, and try to understand the way that it was put together,

25
00:02:31,080 --> 00:02:33,840
and the way that it changed through time as a structure,

26
00:02:34,280 --> 00:02:40,720
then that's through straightforward structural archaeology, through excavation, through the observation of the remains.

27
00:02:44,540 --> 00:02:47,660
Part detective story, part architectural analysis.

28
00:02:48,440 --> 00:02:53,620
What we know of Hadrian's Wall is a saga archaeologists and historians continue to write.

29
00:02:54,620 --> 00:02:58,420
They have learned much of the wall and of the people who live there,

30
00:02:58,820 --> 00:03:01,840
but they have come to realize how much more there is still to learn.

31
00:03:02,820 --> 00:03:04,980
People have been working on Hadrian's Wall for many years,

32
00:03:05,080 --> 00:03:08,580
but I now get the impression that we don't know anything about Hadrian's Wall at all

33
00:03:08,581 --> 00:03:13,440
because every time we excavate a new site or we look at a new survey,

34
00:03:13,441 --> 00:03:19,500
we discover that some of our closely held ideas about Hadrian's Wall don't stand up.

35
00:03:19,501 --> 00:03:24,500
Hadrian's Wall is an imperfect but tantalizing time machine.

36
00:03:24,501 --> 00:03:31,500
With some effort and imagination, it can transport us back to an era we would hardly recognize.

37
00:03:31,501 --> 00:03:38,438
Let's forget immediately that there's an idea of a Britain, as we might understand it as a country or a nation.

38
00:03:38,439 --> 00:03:39,500
It doesn't exist.

39
00:03:39,501 --> 00:03:46,500
So there is a tribe in southeast Britain, there is a tribe a little bit further north, there's another tribe further to the west of that.

40
00:03:46,501 --> 00:03:51,060
and they spend nearly all their time fighting one another, little territorial disputes.

41
00:03:52,260 --> 00:03:54,500
While these local tribes were fighting each other,

42
00:03:55,060 --> 00:03:58,660
farmers eked out a humble living in their iron age roundhouses,

43
00:03:59,300 --> 00:04:01,300
like this recreation in northern England.

44
00:04:02,100 --> 00:04:08,580
Now that means, literally, a roundhouse made of wood, straw, packed with earth,

45
00:04:08,581 --> 00:04:11,196
and with a conical roof, a little like a teepee.

46
00:04:11,197 --> 00:04:13,540
With one entrance, you go through the door,

47
00:04:13,541 --> 00:04:15,800
and you're going to see a huge fire in the middle.

48
00:04:16,340 --> 00:04:19,320
And that fire and the smoke goes up through the roof,

49
00:04:19,620 --> 00:04:23,440
but the heat expands out and keeps the whole place a wonderful warm environment,

50
00:04:23,700 --> 00:04:26,000
dark by our standards and no windows, probably.

51
00:04:27,760 --> 00:04:31,680
These tribal people probably lived this way for hundreds of generations.

52
00:04:32,440 --> 00:04:34,400
A king rises up, he's a powerful man,

53
00:04:34,401 --> 00:04:36,360
the next king in the next tribe is weak,

54
00:04:36,620 --> 00:04:38,600
they have battles, the territory is taken,

55
00:04:39,140 --> 00:04:41,200
another generation passes and it's a different world.

56
00:04:41,380 --> 00:04:43,220
And they spend their time doing that.

57
00:04:43,221 --> 00:04:46,640
all the while aware that the Roman Empire is there across the English Channel.

58
00:04:48,420 --> 00:04:51,380
The Roman Empire was not just across the Channel.

59
00:04:52,180 --> 00:04:56,220
It encompassed all of Europe, south and west of the Rhine and Danube rivers.

60
00:04:56,780 --> 00:04:59,500
It extended east as far as the Caspian Sea,

61
00:05:00,380 --> 00:05:02,960
south into Africa to the sands of the Sahara,

62
00:05:03,500 --> 00:05:05,760
and west to the Atlantic Ocean.

63
00:05:06,480 --> 00:05:10,540
It was an enormous empire that controlled nearly the entire known world,

64
00:05:10,541 --> 00:05:13,380
or at least the world known to the Romans.

65
00:05:15,020 --> 00:05:18,060
By the time Rome began setting its sights on Britain,

66
00:05:18,620 --> 00:05:21,840
it had been a civilization for more than 500 years.

67
00:05:22,860 --> 00:05:25,260
The keys to Rome's unprecedented success

68
00:05:25,261 --> 00:05:27,420
were adaptability and organization.

69
00:05:28,360 --> 00:05:30,540
The Romans excelled at gobbling up cultures

70
00:05:30,541 --> 00:05:32,940
and deftly incorporating them into their empire.

71
00:05:33,440 --> 00:05:36,980
The Romans have an extraordinarily efficient system.

72
00:05:36,981 --> 00:05:40,980
They are the first modern super state.

73
00:05:40,981 --> 00:05:42,980
They have a whole legal order.

74
00:05:42,981 --> 00:05:44,980
They have a whole architectural order.

75
00:05:44,981 --> 00:05:48,980
They have quite simply the most powerful army the world has ever seen.

76
00:05:50,980 --> 00:05:55,980
The Romans showed remarkable tolerance for the local customs and traditions of conquered peoples.

77
00:05:56,980 --> 00:05:59,980
But they also imposed much of their own culture,

78
00:05:59,981 --> 00:06:01,980
which in the long run often proved beneficial.

79
00:06:01,981 --> 00:06:02,681
beneficial.

80
00:06:02,682 --> 00:06:07,580
But if you think about these ordinary people, they had lived in a world in which their

81
00:06:07,581 --> 00:06:12,220
tribal chieftains could take their land, ride roughshod over them, take all their spare produce,

82
00:06:12,500 --> 00:06:14,328
take all the profit from the farm.

83
00:06:14,329 --> 00:06:17,340
When the Romans arrive, they have access to new markets,

84
00:06:17,341 --> 00:06:20,330
to new goods, they have recourse to legal justice.

85
00:06:20,331 --> 00:06:23,260
When they get their estate, it's actually written

86
00:06:23,261 --> 00:06:26,472
down on a docket and it's stored in the public buildings.

87
00:06:26,473 --> 00:06:28,500
Now, there's plenty of corruption in

88
00:06:28,501 --> 00:06:32,880
the Roman world, but one has to recognize that for many people the coming of the Romans

89
00:06:32,881 --> 00:06:37,380
meant a much, much, much better way of life.

90
00:06:37,381 --> 00:06:42,360
The Romans with their rational, organized world view influenced every aspect of the

91
00:06:42,361 --> 00:06:49,420
lives of their subject peoples, from architecture and government to language, even baths.

92
00:06:49,421 --> 00:06:55,080
For the Romans to go to a bath is not just a question of getting washed, it's a question

93
00:06:55,081 --> 00:07:01,000
relaxing in an environment with friends where you steam up, you oil up and you cool off.

94
00:07:01,560 --> 00:07:05,093
The Roman bath is a complex stone structure with vaulted roofs.

95
00:07:05,094 --> 00:07:07,000
You need the vaults because if you

96
00:07:07,001 --> 00:07:09,678
have wooden roofs they'll rot in the steam and fall in.

97
00:07:09,679 --> 00:07:11,480
So you've got the high level of Roman

98
00:07:11,481 --> 00:07:14,254
architecture going into building a Roman bathhouse.

99
00:07:14,255 --> 00:07:16,920
You don't just rush in, have a wash, do your hair

100
00:07:16,921 --> 00:07:18,667
and run off and carry on with the work.

101
00:07:18,668 --> 00:07:21,400
This might be something you would set a large part of the day

102
00:07:21,401 --> 00:07:24,640
a sidefall.

103
00:07:24,641 --> 00:07:27,580
The idea of a sophisticated Roman bath,

104
00:07:27,581 --> 00:07:31,880
such as this reconstruction, in the wilds of cold, forbidding England,

105
00:07:31,881 --> 00:07:35,920
must have seemed crazy to many in the Roman capital.

106
00:07:35,921 --> 00:07:40,580
But the momentum that kept Rome functioning depended on constant expansion,

107
00:07:40,581 --> 00:07:44,060
and England was the next frontier for ambitious emperors,

108
00:07:44,061 --> 00:07:47,700
despite trepidation on the part of soldiers at the front.

109
00:07:47,701 --> 00:07:49,860
We hear of soldiers who were

110
00:07:49,861 --> 00:07:54,560
prepare to mutinier rather than sail to Britain in the early days of conquest.

111
00:07:55,180 --> 00:08:00,700
And even after that, there was always this feeling that Britain was on the edge of things,

112
00:08:00,900 --> 00:08:04,600
that you had to be jolly careful when you got beyond Britain.

113
00:08:06,280 --> 00:08:07,240
You might fall off.

114
00:08:08,660 --> 00:08:14,880
In the year 55 BC, Julius Caesar became the first Roman leader to invade Britain.

115
00:08:15,880 --> 00:08:19,800
It was the beginning of a long, tempestuous relationship with Rome.

116
00:08:19,860 --> 00:08:22,820
that would last for nearly 500 years.

117
00:08:24,100 --> 00:08:27,300
Although Hadrian's Wall is 80 Roman miles long,

118
00:08:27,680 --> 00:08:30,560
a Roman mile is slightly shorter than our modern mile.

119
00:08:31,340 --> 00:08:34,420
Hence, Hadrian's Wall is only 74 miles long.

120
00:08:35,140 --> 00:08:38,160
Hadrian's Wall will return on Modern Marvels.

121
00:08:39,320 --> 00:08:43,000
We now return to Hadrian's Wall on Modern Marvels.

122
00:08:45,920 --> 00:08:47,880
Julius Caesar invaded Britain

123
00:08:47,881 --> 00:08:52,120
because he thought it would be an easy way to bask in the glory of military conquest.

124
00:08:54,920 --> 00:08:59,000
Although he never followed up his earlier invasions with more serious efforts,

125
00:08:59,800 --> 00:09:04,200
one of his successors, the Emperor Claudius, did in AD 43.

126
00:09:06,440 --> 00:09:12,840
As we are specifically told by the Roman writer Suetonius, Claudius required a triumph.

127
00:09:12,841 --> 00:09:16,840
He required military prestige. And of course the same thing happens today.

128
00:09:16,841 --> 00:09:26,520
We see dictators going in for foreign adventures in order to bolster their own domestic position to help pull their country together.

129
00:09:28,340 --> 00:09:32,560
Britain is this supremely exotic place on the end of the world.

130
00:09:32,760 --> 00:09:37,320
Not exotic in the sense that it's full of palm trees and girls with not very many clothes on.

131
00:09:37,560 --> 00:09:40,920
It's exotic in the sense that it's dark and mysterious.

132
00:09:40,921 --> 00:09:44,560
and to win Britain is to show the Roman people

133
00:09:44,561 --> 00:09:49,220
that even now we Romans can capture this place at the end of the world,

134
00:09:49,560 --> 00:09:51,480
even though, in all honesty, it's really worthless,

135
00:09:51,620 --> 00:09:54,240
but it's a mark of our largesse as Roman people

136
00:09:54,241 --> 00:09:58,100
that we can take this place and bring our wonderful civilisation,

137
00:09:58,500 --> 00:09:59,880
because that's the other side of the Romans.

138
00:10:00,380 --> 00:10:03,060
They really believed that they were doing people favours.

139
00:10:04,360 --> 00:10:07,420
The tribal kings didn't quite see things that way

140
00:10:07,421 --> 00:10:10,300
and put up a vigorous, if doomed, resistance.

141
00:10:10,920 --> 00:10:20,460
Over a period of 40 years, aggressive Roman military governors, notably Gnaeus Julius Agricola, were able to subdue most of the island of Britannia.

142
00:10:21,960 --> 00:10:25,520
They were people with their own, if you like, respectable history.

143
00:10:26,280 --> 00:10:28,600
They had some political sophistication.

144
00:10:29,080 --> 00:10:36,700
When threatened by the Romans in the 80s under Agricola, they combined together their forces to fight Agricola.

145
00:10:36,701 --> 00:10:39,920
And although they lost, their instincts were correct.

146
00:10:40,920 --> 00:10:49,080
Now, we know, during the Roman period, that there were treaties that governed the relationship between the Romans and the people beyond the frontier.

147
00:10:49,860 --> 00:10:58,700
The Romans pushed through Britain, establishing larger cities at Colchester and London, and smaller bases in places like York and Chester.

148
00:11:00,300 --> 00:11:05,660
Villages grew up around these bases, with the usual parade of gamblers, prostitutes and vendors.

149
00:11:05,661 --> 00:11:14,660
The Pax Romana, or Roman peace, settled uneasily throughout the land, and soon coins, gods,

150
00:11:14,661 --> 00:11:16,660
and goods filtered through.

151
00:11:16,661 --> 00:11:26,660
I think popular misconception is that it was basically Italians in the army and road painted,

152
00:11:26,661 --> 00:11:31,660
fur clad locals stomping around outside.

153
00:11:31,661 --> 00:11:34,660
This would have been a very, very cosmopolitan area in the Roman period.

154
00:11:34,661 --> 00:11:39,500
period, there were people from all over the Roman world, either in the army or following

155
00:11:39,501 --> 00:11:42,087
the army as merchants and traders.

156
00:11:42,088 --> 00:11:46,120
There were mixed marriages between soldiers and girls

157
00:11:46,121 --> 00:11:48,520
from all over the Roman world.

158
00:11:48,521 --> 00:11:53,160
There would have been goods from all over the Roman world.

159
00:11:53,161 --> 00:11:58,620
At the end of the first century AD, the Roman Empire in Britain extended as far north as

160
00:11:58,621 --> 00:11:59,970
present day Scotland.

161
00:11:59,971 --> 00:12:04,340
Further expansion in Britain was delayed in order to quell uprisings

162
00:12:04,341 --> 00:12:09,300
along the Danube, and this was a pattern that ultimately thwarted a complete occupation

163
00:12:09,301 --> 00:12:11,560
of the island.

164
00:12:11,561 --> 00:12:14,420
But another factor was becoming apparent.

165
00:12:14,421 --> 00:12:19,020
The Roman Empire had reached its maximum sustainable size.

166
00:12:19,021 --> 00:12:22,260
It could no longer simply expand.

167
00:12:22,261 --> 00:12:28,500
Up till now, the idea was that everywhere that Romans had set foot, they stayed.

168
00:12:28,501 --> 00:12:31,440
The Romans could beat anybody around at the time,

169
00:12:32,300 --> 00:12:37,620
and basically went on going until you hit the water or something else, or desert.

170
00:12:38,820 --> 00:12:43,400
It was a very odd idea to stop, and yet, realistically,

171
00:12:43,401 --> 00:12:49,240
and there had to be a point to stop, a point to say, this far and no further.

172
00:12:50,860 --> 00:12:54,000
This was an utterly foreign concept to most Romans,

173
00:12:54,001 --> 00:13:00,000
but one that the Emperor Hadrian, who came to power in AD 117, understood.

174
00:13:00,001 --> 00:13:05,000
Hadrian realized that the Empire could not continue to expand.

175
00:13:05,001 --> 00:13:10,000
It could not put its troops everywhere and continue to control all his frontiers.

176
00:13:10,001 --> 00:13:15,000
Hadrian was a great believer that the Empire had come to its natural limits.

177
00:13:15,001 --> 00:13:19,000
He set the borders. He is the first Roman Emperor to say,

178
00:13:19,001 --> 00:13:21,000
we are going no further.

179
00:13:21,001 --> 00:13:21,906
no further.

180
00:13:21,907 --> 00:13:28,000
Well, Hadrian, he seemed to have the idea that the empire got as big as it

181
00:13:28,001 --> 00:13:29,690
comfortably could be.

182
00:13:29,691 --> 00:13:35,000
And not only in Britain, but in Germany too, and probably in North

183
00:13:35,001 --> 00:13:44,000
Africa, he seemed to go for the idea of establishing a visible frontier, a line.

184
00:13:44,001 --> 00:13:48,592
Born in Spain, Hadrian improved legal administration and the civil service.

185
00:13:48,593 --> 00:13:50,000
He was a lover of Greek

186
00:13:50,001 --> 00:13:55,000
and an accomplished architect who designed the Pantheon in Rome.

187
00:13:55,001 --> 00:14:02,000
His most significant contribution, however, may have been his efforts to keep his soldiers well trained and disciplined,

188
00:14:02,001 --> 00:14:05,000
despite the relative peace under which he ruled.

189
00:14:05,001 --> 00:14:11,000
One way he did this was to put them to work, building barriers along the newly fixed Roman frontier.

190
00:14:11,001 --> 00:14:19,800
In Germany, he cut the corner, really, between Rhine and Danube with just a palisade.

191
00:14:20,320 --> 00:14:25,200
That was enough, it said, where Rome began and the barbarians ended.

192
00:14:25,780 --> 00:14:28,300
And it had the same basic message.

193
00:14:29,480 --> 00:14:31,920
We've got enough. This is ours.

194
00:14:32,740 --> 00:14:35,480
Please knock on the door and wait till somebody opens it.

195
00:14:35,481 --> 00:14:42,480
When he came to Britain, this was perhaps an opportunity for him to experiment with a different kind of architecture,

196
00:14:42,481 --> 00:14:48,480
but we also know that he was very, very displeased with soldiers who weren't doing anything.

197
00:14:48,481 --> 00:14:53,480
He was very bothered about frontier garrisons that were made up of lazy troops.

198
00:14:53,481 --> 00:15:02,480
So it's my personal theory that one of the reasons for Hadrian's War may have been to give the soldiers in Britain something to do.

199
00:15:02,481 --> 00:15:09,480
We know that Hadrian came to Britain to inspect the frontier in A.D. 122.

200
00:15:09,481 --> 00:15:16,480
Almost certainly the idea for the wall and orders to carry it out occurred during this visit.

201
00:15:16,481 --> 00:15:28,879
Most likely, Hadrian saw that the Stain Gate Road, an established trade route between the Solway Firth and the mouth of the River Tyne, was a suitable location for a

202
00:15:28,880 --> 00:15:29,580
barrier.

203
00:15:29,581 --> 00:15:34,720
barrier. Britain is relatively narrow here and natural boundaries like steep

204
00:15:34,721 --> 00:15:39,500
crags, ponds and rivers would augment whatever man-made boundary the soldiers

205
00:15:39,501 --> 00:15:41,980
could build.

206
00:15:42,520 --> 00:15:48,420
Nearby quarries could provide the stone and the existing frontier forts along

207
00:15:48,421 --> 00:15:54,440
the Stain Gate Road could provide men. All of the elements for an ambitious and

208
00:15:54,441 --> 00:15:58,880
unprecedented frontier fortification were in place when Hadrian left Britain

209
00:15:58,881 --> 00:16:04,820
never to return, never to see the wall, which would be his most monumental legacy.

210
00:16:07,060 --> 00:16:09,120
Next, construction begins.

211
00:16:11,040 --> 00:16:16,160
In the early days, most Roman soldiers along Hadrian's Wall came from other Roman territories

212
00:16:16,161 --> 00:16:17,840
such as Northern Europe and Africa.

213
00:16:18,820 --> 00:16:20,140
Few had ever seen Rome.

214
00:16:21,160 --> 00:16:23,980
Hadrian's Wall will continue on Modern Marvels.

215
00:16:23,981 --> 00:16:28,980
We now return to Hadrian's Wall on Modern Marvels.

216
00:16:33,380 --> 00:16:38,760
Hadrian's decision to build a wall along the British frontier marked the start of a new era for the Romans.

217
00:16:39,780 --> 00:16:43,760
Hadrian's Wall, in a sense, is a sort of a defining monument of the Roman Empire.

218
00:16:44,320 --> 00:16:50,520
It represents, first of all, a critical stage when the period of expansion had come to an end

219
00:16:50,521 --> 00:16:54,400
and the empire essentially achieved a defensive posture.

220
00:16:55,400 --> 00:17:00,140
That wasn't necessarily going to continue for every emperor after Hadrian,

221
00:17:00,580 --> 00:17:07,060
but in a sense, Hadrian drew a close to the period of constant expansion.

222
00:17:07,880 --> 00:17:12,220
The northern limit of the empire would be delineated in stone and mortar.

223
00:17:13,800 --> 00:17:19,080
The building of the wall, of course, was a major engineering achievement.

224
00:17:19,081 --> 00:17:23,240
It presumably started with survey parties going out.

225
00:17:23,700 --> 00:17:25,920
We're pretty sure they're using vision a lot.

226
00:17:26,020 --> 00:17:30,840
You can see the way now in which the line of the wall changes on top of a hill

227
00:17:30,841 --> 00:17:36,600
as the surveyors sight through and get their next alignment worked out.

228
00:17:37,160 --> 00:17:40,240
Obviously, they've got to know their general direction, east-west,

229
00:17:40,400 --> 00:17:44,660
but then they've got to do it in terms of a number of short, straight lengths.

230
00:17:44,661 --> 00:17:55,793
To facilitate construction, the wall was divided into sections, which were independently constructed by units of the Roman army, which included among its ranks skilled

231
00:17:55,794 --> 00:17:57,660
builders and other artisans.

232
00:17:57,661 --> 00:18:03,600
Now the ones who name themselves for us are members of the 2nd Legion Augusta, the 6th

233
00:18:03,601 --> 00:18:07,620
Legion Victrix and the 20th Legion Valeria Victrix.

234
00:18:07,621 --> 00:18:10,940
That's the three main legions that formed the garrison of Britain.

235
00:18:10,941 --> 00:18:15,140
But they were probably helped by the auxiliary non-citizen troops as well.

236
00:18:15,141 --> 00:18:19,360
What we know is that they were given blocks of the wall to build and they had to mark

237
00:18:19,361 --> 00:18:23,720
the blocks that they had built, each group of soldiers as a quality control, so that

238
00:18:23,721 --> 00:18:25,240
the Roman army command could say,

239
00:18:25,440 --> 00:18:27,760
that is one hell of a bad bit of war,

240
00:18:28,120 --> 00:18:31,340
and there's a stone at the end that actually says who built it,

241
00:18:31,700 --> 00:18:34,420
and the guy who was responsible for that could be toasted for it.

242
00:18:35,680 --> 00:18:39,160
As originally conceived, the wall was to be made of stone

243
00:18:39,161 --> 00:18:42,260
for the easternmost two-thirds, as in this reconstruction,

244
00:18:43,100 --> 00:18:46,420
with turf blocks and timber making up the remaining third.

245
00:18:49,580 --> 00:18:51,540
Solid turf was cut from the ground

246
00:18:51,541 --> 00:18:56,200
into surprisingly strong and stable blocks and stacked up to build a wall.

247
00:18:57,540 --> 00:19:02,180
The wall was studded at regular intervals along its south side with fortified gates,

248
00:19:02,720 --> 00:19:06,620
known as milecastles, which allowed limited access through the wall.

249
00:19:13,340 --> 00:19:18,700
Two turrets ascending above the wall proper would be evenly spaced between each milecastle.

250
00:19:18,701 --> 00:19:25,440
Men from already established forts behind the wall served shifts at the border.

251
00:19:27,120 --> 00:19:32,160
Beyond the wall to the north, a ditch ten feet deep would be dug as an additional barrier,

252
00:19:32,680 --> 00:19:35,880
except in places where the natural landscape made it unnecessary.

253
00:19:37,820 --> 00:19:42,300
In three locations, the wall crossed rivers as fortified bridges.

254
00:19:43,040 --> 00:19:48,080
You start counting up in terms of millions of stones used in the wall,

255
00:19:48,081 --> 00:19:53,260
and, of course, the business of building this over 70-odd miles.

256
00:19:54,040 --> 00:19:56,080
One wonders, really, how many people were killed

257
00:19:56,081 --> 00:19:57,920
in the process of building Hadrian's Wall.

258
00:19:58,780 --> 00:20:01,180
I wish we knew how long it would take them to build it.

259
00:20:01,220 --> 00:20:02,560
It's very difficult to imagine.

260
00:20:03,740 --> 00:20:05,120
It would have taken several years,

261
00:20:05,180 --> 00:20:07,460
but we know that they kept changing their minds

262
00:20:07,461 --> 00:20:08,700
about what they were going to have,

263
00:20:08,800 --> 00:20:11,080
and they were still putting in forts

264
00:20:11,081 --> 00:20:14,080
and tweaking bits and pieces of it in 133 AD.

265
00:20:14,081 --> 00:20:20,400
The decision was made to convert the turf wall in the west into stone.

266
00:20:20,900 --> 00:20:24,240
But the new wall did not exactly follow the path of the old one.

267
00:20:25,080 --> 00:20:28,140
The stone wall was originally planned to be 10 feet thick,

268
00:20:28,740 --> 00:20:31,000
but that dimension diminished to 8 feet,

269
00:20:31,400 --> 00:20:35,360
and even 6 as work progressed, possibly to economize.

270
00:20:36,960 --> 00:20:40,660
Later, it was decided to build a number of forts directly next to the wall,

271
00:20:40,661 --> 00:20:44,280
rather than to rely only on the existing forts to the south.

272
00:20:45,380 --> 00:20:47,880
This required major reworking of the wall.

273
00:20:48,880 --> 00:20:51,560
Even later, another ditch, called a valum,

274
00:20:51,820 --> 00:20:54,460
was dug 10 feet deep and 20 feet wide,

275
00:20:54,740 --> 00:20:56,600
with mounds on both sides.

276
00:20:57,180 --> 00:20:59,860
But this ditch was to the south, or inside the wall,

277
00:21:00,100 --> 00:21:01,800
not the north or frontier side.

278
00:21:02,500 --> 00:21:04,280
So it creates a sort of wall zone

279
00:21:04,940 --> 00:21:07,980
with quite strict control on people coming into it

280
00:21:07,981 --> 00:21:10,440
from the south, from the province itself.

281
00:21:10,660 --> 00:21:14,500
as well as, of course, to protect the control from the north.

282
00:21:15,300 --> 00:21:18,940
So it's as if the situation's changed a bit,

283
00:21:19,160 --> 00:21:21,540
and they're just that little bit more wary.

284
00:21:22,200 --> 00:21:25,780
If you had some nasty, inclined people,

285
00:21:26,240 --> 00:21:29,720
they could walk up to a tower and cut the throats of the people in it,

286
00:21:29,820 --> 00:21:32,720
which wouldn't, on the whole, make for good relationships.

287
00:21:34,520 --> 00:21:38,740
Late in the process, the Romans decided to build a road south of the wall,

288
00:21:38,741 --> 00:21:41,660
but inside the wall zone created by the Valum.

289
00:21:42,320 --> 00:21:46,120
This military way connected the forts, milecastles, and turrets.

290
00:21:48,540 --> 00:21:51,360
Did the wall make an impression on the local farmers

291
00:21:51,361 --> 00:21:54,440
as they watched it twist and curl like a snake

292
00:21:54,441 --> 00:21:56,560
over the hills and valleys further west?

293
00:22:01,300 --> 00:22:04,340
Did it demonstrate for them the strength of the empire?

294
00:22:05,780 --> 00:22:07,700
There were more impressive structures in Rome,

295
00:22:07,701 --> 00:22:11,180
marble temples and amphitheaters, delicate gardens.

296
00:22:14,240 --> 00:22:17,080
Hadrian's wall was rough, blunt power.

297
00:22:18,080 --> 00:22:20,520
Now the wall itself was really quite simple,

298
00:22:20,960 --> 00:22:25,700
at times almost, one might say, clumsy.

299
00:22:25,840 --> 00:22:30,020
It's not a great piece of work because it wasn't intended to be finished off

300
00:22:30,021 --> 00:22:33,760
as one of the great monuments of Rome or Athens.

301
00:22:33,761 --> 00:22:39,440
but it was a good workman-like achievement.

302
00:22:40,640 --> 00:22:42,700
An engineer's work rather than architect.

303
00:22:44,040 --> 00:22:46,120
By the middle of the second century,

304
00:22:46,600 --> 00:22:49,020
a visitor to the frontier traveling from the south

305
00:22:49,021 --> 00:22:50,520
would have been stopped by the Valor.

306
00:22:50,521 --> 00:23:04,521
...based all four sides.

307
00:23:05,980 --> 00:23:09,020
Each fort contained barracks for the men of the garrison,

308
00:23:09,740 --> 00:23:15,320
the home of the commanding officer, workshops, storehouses, and a headquarters building.

309
00:23:15,321 --> 00:23:24,160
This building was the focus of the fort, which contained administrative offices and a shrine.

310
00:23:24,161 --> 00:23:28,680
Outside the fort, a civilian settlement dependent on trade from the soldiers would likely have

311
00:23:28,681 --> 00:23:34,480
grown up, including row houses, an inn, taverns and shops.

312
00:23:34,481 --> 00:23:40,120
The bath would also have been found outside the fort, as would the cemetery.

313
00:23:40,121 --> 00:23:44,600
Traveling along the military way, a visitor would have come across turrets rising above

314
00:23:44,601 --> 00:23:50,980
the wall with a second story and access to the wall top. Only a handful of men would

315
00:23:50,981 --> 00:23:56,940
be stationed at a turret, which would have provided a view of the surrounding countryside.

316
00:23:56,941 --> 00:24:01,920
Further down would be a mile castle, again rising to the height of the wall and containing

317
00:24:01,921 --> 00:24:03,708
a stairway to the wall top.

318
00:24:03,709 --> 00:24:07,880
The square-shaped mile castle would have included a center road

319
00:24:07,881 --> 00:24:10,075
leading through the wall gate.

320
00:24:10,076 --> 00:24:14,100
Other small buildings and housing for about a dozen men

321
00:24:14,101 --> 00:24:16,700
indicate a larger but still modest force.

322
00:24:17,540 --> 00:24:20,720
And then the wall itself, reconstructed here,

323
00:24:21,360 --> 00:24:23,840
likely rising to a height of about 15 feet.

324
00:24:24,640 --> 00:24:28,060
But there is much that we still don't know about the wall even today.

325
00:24:29,160 --> 00:24:31,420
We know about how wide the wall was, how it was built,

326
00:24:31,760 --> 00:24:34,000
but what happened at the top is open to discussion.

327
00:24:34,620 --> 00:24:36,940
Some people think it was crenellated,

328
00:24:37,280 --> 00:24:38,740
others think it was flat,

329
00:24:38,860 --> 00:24:42,860
others think it was shaped as a slope so the rain fell off it.

330
00:24:42,861 --> 00:24:44,900
At the moment, we're not totally clear.

331
00:24:45,940 --> 00:24:51,040
One of the stereotypes which is most difficult to take out of our minds

332
00:24:51,041 --> 00:24:54,200
is the fact that Hadrian's Wall was manned by soldiers

333
00:24:54,201 --> 00:24:57,900
who lined up along the top to repel attack.

334
00:24:59,260 --> 00:25:04,280
Firstly, the wall itself militates against this.

335
00:25:04,920 --> 00:25:09,260
The top is actually quite narrow, so it's not easy to move along it,

336
00:25:09,560 --> 00:25:11,960
assuming that it was possible to move along it.

337
00:25:11,961 --> 00:25:17,140
I think if you have a wall and you can't really get on top of it, you're limiting your possibilities.

338
00:25:18,320 --> 00:25:21,880
Of course, another problem relating to the appearance of, was it painted?

339
00:25:22,760 --> 00:25:30,240
Well, the excavations I carried out, it was only one stone, but that stone certainly seemed to indicate that it had been painted.

340
00:25:30,420 --> 00:25:37,320
I personally think that this is just the line mortar from between the stones being leached down by rainwater.

341
00:25:37,321 --> 00:25:46,320
Whatever its ultimate shape, size or color, by the middle of the second century, Hadrian's

342
00:25:46,321 --> 00:25:51,880
Wall was complete, solid and functioning, clearly marking the furthest reaches of the

343
00:25:51,881 --> 00:25:53,940
Roman Empire.

344
00:25:53,941 --> 00:25:58,560
Having accomplished this remarkable feat, the Romans under a new emperor moved their

345
00:25:58,561 --> 00:26:04,200
border 100 miles further north and largely abandoned Hadrian's Wall.

346
00:26:04,201 --> 00:26:08,300
Next, the Antonine Wall, made entirely of turf and timber.

347
00:26:10,640 --> 00:26:14,300
Beyond the westernmost portion of Hadrian's Wall in the Irish Sea,

348
00:26:14,720 --> 00:26:18,260
the system of milecastles and turrets continued along the seashore,

349
00:26:18,660 --> 00:26:20,760
even though no wall was ever built there.

350
00:26:21,240 --> 00:26:23,800
Hadrian's Wall will return on Modern Marvels.

351
00:26:25,000 --> 00:26:28,400
We now return to Hadrian's Wall on Modern Marvels.

352
00:26:28,401 --> 00:26:36,800
Antoninus Pius, Hadrian's successor, continued Rome's tradition of expansion

353
00:26:36,801 --> 00:26:42,780
and pushed the border of Rome's northern frontier even further north into present-day Scotland,

354
00:26:43,020 --> 00:26:47,360
where he built a turf and timber wall known as the Antonine Wall

355
00:26:47,361 --> 00:26:51,580
that ran for 40 miles roughly between modern Edinburgh and Glasgow.

356
00:26:52,940 --> 00:26:55,540
Now, of course, the important question is, why did this happen?

357
00:26:55,541 --> 00:27:03,280
We used to think that it was perhaps because of warfare north of Hadrian's Wall which entailed a forward move of the frontier.

358
00:27:04,280 --> 00:27:10,280
But more recent thought has suggested that it may be simply no more than the fact that the new emperor,

359
00:27:10,760 --> 00:27:18,640
who had never served in the army, wanted to improve or rather establish his military credentials by a victory.

360
00:27:19,420 --> 00:27:22,320
Obviously the wall hasn't got the same structure as a stone wall.

361
00:27:22,321 --> 00:27:27,220
has to be much wider, and it doesn't have a sheer face to the outsides.

362
00:27:27,840 --> 00:27:30,060
But it can still represent a formidable barrier.

363
00:27:30,600 --> 00:27:34,180
And this, in a sense, was the traditional Roman military building material.

364
00:27:35,080 --> 00:27:40,040
But within 15 or 20 years, the Romans returned to occupy Hadrian's Wall

365
00:27:40,041 --> 00:27:42,080
as the main demarcation of the empire,

366
00:27:42,880 --> 00:27:46,480
abandoning the area north except for a handful of frontier forts.

367
00:27:47,480 --> 00:27:51,240
And the abandonment of Hadrian's Wall for that brief period

368
00:27:51,241 --> 00:27:53,220
and the building of the Antonine Wall,

369
00:27:53,580 --> 00:27:57,320
they may be no more than a pawn in this much wider

370
00:27:57,321 --> 00:28:02,460
and for the Emperor Antoninus Pius, obviously, more significant game.

371
00:28:03,140 --> 00:28:05,940
It is wrong, however, to think of Hadrian's Wall

372
00:28:05,941 --> 00:28:09,180
as simply a bulwark against rowdy barbarian hordes.

373
00:28:09,980 --> 00:28:12,680
Its purpose was multifaceted and complex.

374
00:28:13,180 --> 00:28:14,520
It's not a castle wall.

375
00:28:14,940 --> 00:28:16,800
It's not really wide enough to fight from,

376
00:28:16,880 --> 00:28:19,340
and the Roman army didn't do that sort of fighting.

377
00:28:19,341 --> 00:28:25,120
what they preferred to do was to get out on the ground and round you up,

378
00:28:25,121 --> 00:28:31,500
give you a thrashing, generally give you the impression that you didn't mess with the Romans.

379
00:28:32,400 --> 00:28:37,640
So from their point of view, they didn't want to stand up on the wall and wave spears of people.

380
00:28:37,900 --> 00:28:43,180
They wanted to go out through all those gates and get to close quarters with anybody

381
00:28:43,181 --> 00:28:46,500
who needed education about the Roman peace.

382
00:28:46,501 --> 00:28:52,260
But the people on the far side of the wall weren't necessarily armed attackers.

383
00:28:52,261 --> 00:28:58,300
There were long peaceful periods in which people and trade certainly moved back and forth through the wall.

384
00:28:58,301 --> 00:29:02,180
But such contact and communication needed to be controlled,

385
00:29:02,181 --> 00:29:05,980
because those coming from the north were from beyond the empire,

386
00:29:05,981 --> 00:29:07,780
and they were potentially hostile.

387
00:29:07,781 --> 00:29:12,500
You see, the way to understand it best is to think if there wasn't a wall,

388
00:29:12,501 --> 00:29:20,080
However carefully you patrol, however much you have soldiers on the ground, somebody can slip through.

389
00:29:20,600 --> 00:29:25,660
You can raid, you can infiltrate, you can make a new suit of yourselves.

390
00:29:26,620 --> 00:29:36,720
But once you've got a wall there, you try and go cattle lifting and you find you've got to lift your cattle over a 15 foot high wall.

391
00:29:36,721 --> 00:29:47,940
You want to bring all your booty back, you put on wagons and you find you've got to go through a gate where somebody asks where you got all that from.

392
00:29:48,540 --> 00:29:52,480
And if we take a modern analogy, we can see this very clearly with the Berlin Wall.

393
00:29:52,840 --> 00:30:00,560
The Berlin Wall clearly stopped people moving across it, but it would not have stopped a major invasion one way or the other.

394
00:30:00,660 --> 00:30:05,320
It would have held up the tanks for a little while, but otherwise it wouldn't have stopped them.

395
00:30:05,321 --> 00:30:08,080
It was, if you like, the edge of Caesar's land.

396
00:30:08,200 --> 00:30:12,980
It allowed the Romans to control people moving in and out of the province,

397
00:30:13,220 --> 00:30:17,100
but it wasn't there to protect the province as such.

398
00:30:17,300 --> 00:30:20,580
That was done by the soldiers basing the forts on the wall.

399
00:30:22,480 --> 00:30:27,500
Those soldiers stationed at each turret and milecastle at every fort along the wall

400
00:30:27,501 --> 00:30:30,380
brought a sense of continuity to the frontier

401
00:30:30,381 --> 00:30:35,000
as generation after generation of men lived and died.

402
00:30:35,320 --> 00:30:37,200
on the far edge of the Roman Empire.

403
00:30:39,880 --> 00:30:42,300
Auxiliary soldiers stationed near the wall

404
00:30:42,301 --> 00:30:44,820
included mixed infantry and cavalry regiments,

405
00:30:45,120 --> 00:30:46,800
which consisted of 500 men.

406
00:30:48,900 --> 00:30:52,700
Infantry-only regiments of 500 men were also stationed there,

407
00:30:53,180 --> 00:30:55,840
as were mixed and infantry cohorts,

408
00:30:56,220 --> 00:30:58,360
which contained twice as many men as a regiment,

409
00:30:58,820 --> 00:31:00,500
at 1,000 men strong.

410
00:31:00,501 --> 00:31:05,940
Cavalry regiments were somewhat rare.

411
00:31:07,380 --> 00:31:11,560
Men who enlisted in the Roman army were required to remain for 25 years,

412
00:31:11,980 --> 00:31:13,780
which was not the hardship it seems.

413
00:31:15,060 --> 00:31:20,180
You're assured of food, of clothing, of medical care,

414
00:31:20,181 --> 00:31:21,360
you mustn't forget that,

415
00:31:21,860 --> 00:31:24,040
and you've got a pretty good expectation of life

416
00:31:24,041 --> 00:31:26,660
compared with the people outside the army,

417
00:31:26,820 --> 00:31:30,380
which may seem funny, but an army is a very safe place in peacetime.

418
00:31:30,500 --> 00:31:34,360
You know, you went through the motions, you did your basic training,

419
00:31:34,960 --> 00:31:37,580
you drank with your mates, you had a bit of a gamble,

420
00:31:38,160 --> 00:31:41,540
and at the end of that time, while you were a Roman citizen,

421
00:31:41,940 --> 00:31:43,440
and your children were Roman citizens,

422
00:31:44,120 --> 00:31:46,640
and probably when you'd done your 25 years,

423
00:31:47,060 --> 00:31:49,080
all you did was walk down the hill,

424
00:31:50,300 --> 00:31:52,080
take a house in the civil settlement,

425
00:31:52,081 --> 00:31:57,400
and yarn with the lads about how things were when you were in the army.

426
00:31:57,401 --> 00:32:05,620
after a while the army was likely able to recruit the sons of soldiers who retired outside the fort

427
00:32:05,621 --> 00:32:12,240
to the local civil settlement or vicus this pattern probably continued for hundreds of years

428
00:32:12,241 --> 00:32:19,160
so that the frontier was not only an assignment it was home for those stationed there let's imagine

429
00:32:19,161 --> 00:32:24,780
that we're arriving at a roman fort and we don't just go into the roman fort we go through a whole

430
00:32:24,781 --> 00:32:28,765
civilian community outside the fort servicing the Roman army.

431
00:32:28,766 --> 00:32:30,660
This is where bars are, where

432
00:32:30,661 --> 00:32:35,300
brothels are, where all sorts of activities are going on, perhaps even the soldiers' wives

433
00:32:35,301 --> 00:32:37,251
and families living outside.

434
00:32:37,252 --> 00:32:41,500
Within the fort itself we have a superbly organised structure

435
00:32:41,501 --> 00:32:46,673
with gates, fortifications and inside the barrack buildings a hospital.

436
00:32:46,674 --> 00:32:47,620
Every day the

437
00:32:47,621 --> 00:32:50,127
soldiers here are dealing with normal fatigues.

438
00:32:50,128 --> 00:32:52,580
They've got a march, they've got to sort their

439
00:32:52,581 --> 00:32:57,580
equipment out. They also have some leisure time.

440
00:32:57,581 --> 00:33:01,580
There's going to be the smell from blacksmiths.

441
00:33:01,581 --> 00:33:03,764
And they're also going to be the prostitutes.

442
00:33:03,765 --> 00:33:06,580
One thing you can guarantee, that wherever a soldier goes,

443
00:33:06,581 --> 00:33:12,580
the oldest profession in the world goes with it. It's an absolute fact of life.

444
00:33:12,581 --> 00:33:21,580
So this is a whole thriving underclass world of commerce and prostitution and ordinary families.

445
00:33:21,581 --> 00:33:27,480
It's really the whole of humanity, but it's driven by the Roman army with those soldiers with the pay.

446
00:33:27,720 --> 00:33:34,840
Which is why when we get to the end of the Roman world and the pay stops arriving for the soldiers on Hadrian's Wall, these communities disappear.

447
00:33:36,540 --> 00:33:41,180
Britain was, after all, on the outskirts of the empire, on the fringe.

448
00:33:42,600 --> 00:33:50,140
In 306, Emperor Constantine took the throne and later moved the center of Roman civilization eastward to Constantinople.

449
00:33:51,580 --> 00:33:59,380
Slowly but inexorably, the Roman Empire was falling apart.

450
00:33:59,381 --> 00:34:06,020
By then, soldiers on the frontier were as much British as Roman, maybe more so.

451
00:34:06,021 --> 00:34:10,020
The world was passing Hadrian's Wall by.

452
00:34:10,021 --> 00:34:15,180
In the civil settlement at Howstead's Roman Fort, archaeologists discovered evidence of

453
00:34:15,181 --> 00:34:21,140
an ancient double murder hidden beneath the foundation of a longhouse.

454
00:34:21,141 --> 00:34:23,480
Hadrian's Wall will return on Modern Marvels.

455
00:34:24,560 --> 00:34:28,500
We now return to Hadrian's Wall on Modern Marvels.

456
00:34:30,040 --> 00:34:34,340
In 410 A.D., Rome officially abandoned Britain.

457
00:34:36,340 --> 00:34:37,940
Decline began years earlier.

458
00:34:39,300 --> 00:34:42,160
The number of men on the frontier had certainly been reduced.

459
00:34:43,780 --> 00:34:48,700
There was no mass exodus to Rome by soldiers who had grown up in the shadow of the wall.

460
00:34:48,701 --> 00:34:53,140
There was no great battle to wrest the island from the Romans.

461
00:34:54,240 --> 00:34:57,140
But Britain was gradually left to its own devices,

462
00:34:57,820 --> 00:35:01,400
until finally, after over 350 years of Roman rule,

463
00:35:01,920 --> 00:35:03,280
it was on its own again.

464
00:35:04,700 --> 00:35:09,820
Hadrian's Wall at the end is a ramshackle, Mad Max world,

465
00:35:10,200 --> 00:35:12,580
of propped-up gates, which are fallen over,

466
00:35:12,920 --> 00:35:15,300
repaired with bits of wood, blocked up.

467
00:35:15,301 --> 00:35:18,300
What's probably the case is that we may have communities

468
00:35:18,301 --> 00:35:21,300
that are starting to control the local area

469
00:35:21,301 --> 00:35:24,300
a little bit like those tribal chieftains

470
00:35:24,301 --> 00:35:27,300
all those years ago before the Romans came.

471
00:35:27,301 --> 00:35:29,300
And then when the system finally collapses,

472
00:35:29,301 --> 00:35:31,300
we may have these military communities

473
00:35:31,301 --> 00:35:35,300
turning into the sort of groups resembling those tribes

474
00:35:35,301 --> 00:35:37,300
that we had in Britain beforehand,

475
00:35:37,301 --> 00:35:40,300
controlling little pockets of territory.

476
00:35:40,301 --> 00:35:48,765
All that Rome established began to fall away. Lawlessness and illiteracy crept back.

477
00:35:48,766 --> 00:35:53,300
And forgetfulness. The wall began to crumble.

478
00:35:53,301 --> 00:35:58,603
And of course it remained a boundary. Farmers observed it.

479
00:35:58,604 --> 00:36:09,300
It was never actually a boundary between England and Scotland, but it was a field boundary and continued in that way.

480
00:36:09,301 --> 00:36:17,240
The wall became a quarry as people would remove stones to build farmhouses or fences or even early churches.

481
00:36:18,360 --> 00:36:26,440
As Christianity, the last great gift of the Roman Empire, had made its way to the furthest reaches of the once great civilization.

482
00:36:29,040 --> 00:36:33,080
Knowledge of the Roman presence in Britain was soon nearly totally lost.

483
00:36:33,081 --> 00:36:44,580
The venerable Bede, early father of English history, lived in his monastic cell in Newcastle in the 7th century, only 200 years after the Romans left.

484
00:36:44,581 --> 00:36:54,860
now when he looked out of his cell window in the 620s thereabouts he could see the wall i mean you

485
00:36:54,861 --> 00:36:59,200
know it would have been standing to its full height then and in his description of hadion's

486
00:36:59,201 --> 00:37:05,000
wall he relied on information he got from other people and told us that the wall was built in the

487
00:37:05,001 --> 00:37:10,640
middle of the fifth century which is after the romans have gone from britain so you know there's

488
00:37:10,641 --> 00:37:16,120
Someone, an intelligent man, living relatively close to the period, didn't know anything about it.

489
00:37:18,580 --> 00:37:24,200
Dilapidated forts became headquarters for roving bands of local highwaymen, called reavers.

490
00:37:25,300 --> 00:37:28,060
Some mile castles were converted to bastel houses,

491
00:37:28,640 --> 00:37:32,800
or fortified farmhouses with animals on the ground level and living quarters above.

492
00:37:34,000 --> 00:37:36,160
Over the next thousand years,

493
00:37:36,161 --> 00:37:42,200
as territorial battles between England and Scotland played themselves out on the slopes and crags of Northumberland.

494
00:37:42,960 --> 00:37:47,100
Nearly all the structures slowly collapsed, except the wall itself.

495
00:37:54,260 --> 00:38:00,940
When stability returned to the region in the 17th century, estates were granted and tenant farmers returned.

496
00:38:00,941 --> 00:38:06,180
and interest in the ancient wall began to rekindle among the historically inclined,

497
00:38:06,980 --> 00:38:09,500
although not all of the early deductions were accurate.

498
00:38:10,640 --> 00:38:13,360
Christopher Ridley, the curate of the local church in Holtweswald,

499
00:38:13,840 --> 00:38:17,900
he reported, for example, that Roman chariots driving along Hadrian's Wall,

500
00:38:17,901 --> 00:38:21,700
well, anyone who's seen Hadrian's Wall and realized the ups and downs of the wall

501
00:38:21,701 --> 00:38:24,680
would know that they'd have to have steps in the wall, for God's sake,

502
00:38:25,060 --> 00:38:27,120
and no chariot would possibly get along it.

503
00:38:27,121 --> 00:38:32,240
He also had the idea that they had a speaking tube, a bronze speaking tube,

504
00:38:32,280 --> 00:38:34,860
all the way along the wall for talking to each other,

505
00:38:35,280 --> 00:38:40,520
and claimed that this bronze speaking tube was still maintained by the local bigwigs today.

506
00:38:41,440 --> 00:38:42,460
Absolute drivel.

507
00:38:44,240 --> 00:38:46,380
People began to explore the wall.

508
00:38:47,520 --> 00:38:52,320
Occasionally, a public works program would dislodge and reveal a portion previously unseen.

509
00:38:53,220 --> 00:38:59,700
By the late 19th century, a group of educated historians and boosters began to excavate along the wall.

510
00:39:01,680 --> 00:39:06,480
I think the most important person for the history of excavation on Hadrian's Wall was John Clayton.

511
00:39:07,000 --> 00:39:10,320
He was very interested in archaeology because his family home was at Chester's.

512
00:39:10,720 --> 00:39:12,160
And this seemed to have got him very interested.

513
00:39:12,360 --> 00:39:16,700
And over the years, he bought up stretches of Hadrian's Wall as it became available.

514
00:39:17,400 --> 00:39:18,920
And he excavated quite a number of them.

515
00:39:18,921 --> 00:39:23,920
Artifacts began to find their way into museums.

516
00:39:23,921 --> 00:39:28,920
Discoveries began to make their way into the news.

517
00:39:28,921 --> 00:39:33,920
Hadrian's Wall was rescued from the brink of obscurity.

518
00:39:36,920 --> 00:39:42,920
John Clayton's collection of artifacts found along the wall can still be seen in the museum built near his home,

519
00:39:42,921 --> 00:39:45,920
at the Roman fort of Chester's.

520
00:39:45,921 --> 00:39:50,360
Built shortly after his death in 1890 at age 98.

521
00:39:52,680 --> 00:39:57,680
Excavation and preservation throughout the 20th century have salvaged much of what was left.

522
00:39:59,140 --> 00:40:04,740
In 1987, Hadrian's Wall was named a World Heritage Site by UNESCO,

523
00:40:05,260 --> 00:40:08,820
the United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization.

524
00:40:08,821 --> 00:40:18,140
As it once did for the Romans, Hadrian's Wall continues to function in a number of capacities.

525
00:40:19,760 --> 00:40:26,500
It is a tourist attraction for visitors to the north of England, and it is an archaeological treasure trove.

526
00:40:27,520 --> 00:40:32,660
Robin Burley has spent much of the 30-plus years he's been excavating the Roman fort of Vindolanda,

527
00:40:32,661 --> 00:40:35,560
extracting delicate papyrus documents

528
00:40:35,561 --> 00:40:39,660
upon which all sorts of information was written by the men stationed there.

529
00:40:40,380 --> 00:40:43,660
One of the first documents we got was a private letter

530
00:40:43,661 --> 00:40:47,620
coming in from one lady, the wife as it turned out of another commanding officer

531
00:40:47,621 --> 00:40:49,820
to our commanding officer's wife

532
00:40:49,821 --> 00:40:54,340
inviting her to a birthday party on the 11th of September

533
00:40:54,341 --> 00:40:58,140
in a year wasn't mentioned but it's probably 103, 104 AD.

534
00:40:58,141 --> 00:41:06,020
Now, that was the first hint that even the earliest garrisons up here, they brought their wives and families with them.

535
00:41:07,280 --> 00:41:10,620
Not all of Burley's discoveries are as dramatic as that, however.

536
00:41:11,940 --> 00:41:16,560
Now, you know, the odd person watching us excavate when we bring up a boot or a shoe thinks,

537
00:41:16,680 --> 00:41:17,460
why, that's exciting.

538
00:41:18,420 --> 00:41:23,940
My record number of boots and shoes is 75 in a day, which they chuck into old ditches.

539
00:41:24,000 --> 00:41:25,000
You see, they never disintegrate.

540
00:41:25,200 --> 00:41:26,340
You can't burn leather.

541
00:41:27,020 --> 00:41:27,840
They're all hanging about.

542
00:41:28,520 --> 00:41:30,660
We have on display in our museum here

543
00:41:30,661 --> 00:41:33,340
the largest collection of footwear in the Roman world.

544
00:41:33,440 --> 00:41:36,800
We've got about 140 boots, shoes, sandals, slippers on display,

545
00:41:37,560 --> 00:41:38,360
which looks terrific.

546
00:41:38,960 --> 00:41:40,800
I've got another 2,500 upstairs.

547
00:41:41,480 --> 00:41:44,180
Part of being an archaeologist along Hadrian's Wall

548
00:41:44,181 --> 00:41:47,260
means being willing to jettison long-held beliefs

549
00:41:47,261 --> 00:41:50,140
in the face of recently unearthed evidence to the contrary.

550
00:41:51,340 --> 00:41:55,240
Every year also, one or two of the cherished theories of the past

551
00:41:55,241 --> 00:41:56,040
are demolished.

552
00:41:56,340 --> 00:41:58,863
something new appears. What on earth does that mean?

553
00:41:58,864 --> 00:42:00,900
And you know, you've got to go back to the

554
00:42:00,901 --> 00:42:03,478
drawing board and try and work it all out.

555
00:42:03,479 --> 00:42:07,100
I don't think more than 5% of the whole thing has ever been

556
00:42:07,101 --> 00:42:12,340
researched, and we'll still be researching Hadrian's Wall in a thousand years' time.

557
00:42:12,341 --> 00:42:26,341
Burleigh recently uncovered row upon row of traditional roundhouses within the fort of

558
00:42:26,740 --> 00:42:27,763
Vindolanda.

559
00:42:27,764 --> 00:42:35,020
These houses have never been found within a Roman fort before, and speculation

560
00:42:35,021 --> 00:42:41,180
suggests they may have been used to house barbarian hostages during an uprising.

561
00:42:45,180 --> 00:42:51,660
There is still much we can learn from the wall if we bend and listen closely.

562
00:42:51,661 --> 00:42:58,460
Nothing lasts two thousand years without imparting some insight on survival.

563
00:42:58,461 --> 00:43:00,460
Persistence.

564
00:43:00,461 --> 00:43:04,100
Adaptability.

565
00:43:04,101 --> 00:43:08,500
I think the wall then represents this phase in which Britain suddenly becomes part of

566
00:43:08,501 --> 00:43:13,000
a much greater European culture.

567
00:43:13,001 --> 00:43:18,820
It's obviously been part of that in prehistoric terms, but at that, in those few centuries,

568
00:43:18,821 --> 00:43:24,400
it becomes a significant element of that system.

569
00:43:24,401 --> 00:43:28,780
And it reminds us of living today that we are part of not just a single island, but

570
00:43:28,781 --> 00:43:32,020
of a much greater community.

571
00:43:32,021 --> 00:43:39,200
The way the wall no longer separates, it helps us bridge the gap between then and now, to

572
00:43:39,201 --> 00:43:44,800
teach us that we are part of one community.

573
00:43:44,801 --> 00:43:51,880
The community of man, living and dead.

574
00:43:51,881 --> 00:43:54,360
It still strides across the landscape.

575
00:43:54,361 --> 00:44:01,660
It still gives you a feeling of the might of the greatest empire that the world has

576
00:44:01,661 --> 00:44:04,421
ever seen the Roman Empire.

577
00:44:04,422 --> 00:44:10,760
And it's justifiably the most famous, the most well-known, and

578
00:44:10,761 --> 00:44:14,180
dare I say, the best love Roman frontier surviving today.

